  "query_preview": "..There's an old saying that was passed down to me\nfrom my grandma, \"after a storm comes the calm.\" While today \nmay not be entirely calm with a slight chance for light showers \nremaining along the Arizona/New Mexico border this afternoon, \nconsiderably quieter weather is in store for Southeast Arizona \nthe next several days following our recent bout with turbulent \nweather.\n\nThe upper weather pattern this afternoon is defined by a mean\ntrough across the NRN Intermountain West extending SSW into \nsouthern California...with the latest water vapor imagery \nindicating two distinct low centers across northern Nevada and \ncentral California. The recent GOES precipitable water imagery \nindicated a significant area of drier air has expanded into the \narea, with PWATs between 0.40 and 0.50 across Pima and Santa \nCruz counties. Still some lingering moisture holding onto the \nArizona/New Mexico border with PWATs ranging between 0.65 and \n0.75 inches. This is supported by surface dewpoints early this \nafternoon, with values hovering from the mid 40s across far \nwestern Pima to the lower 50s in the Tucson Metro, and in the \nupper 50s in the Gila River Valley and along the AZ/NM state \nline. I would expect decent mixing this afternoon will bring \nthe dewpoints in Tucson down into the mid 40s, but I am confident\nthat higher moisture will persist out east, especially given \nthe higher soil moisture content. The combination of weak mid- \nlevel lift east of the mean trough and this moisture along the \nAZ/NM border will result in a slight chance for light showers \nthis afternoon, possibly persisting into mid-day Saturday. \nOtherwise, expect fair weather cumulus from Tucson east today \nand clear skies west.\n\nThe upper-low to our west eventually ejects NE this weekend with\nanother kicker low developing along the Pacific Northwest \ncoastline. Ensemble members are all over the place with regards \nto both the north/south and east/west position and movement of \nthis feature with lots of variance evident on the Empirical \nOrthogonal Functions (EOF) of the GEFS/ENS/GEPS Sunday. In \ngeneral we will see an upper-low develop, but the amplification \nand position will depend on the strength of the upper ridge \nbuilding into the Gulf of Alaska. This has ramifications for \nwhat happens later next week, as both the deterministic GFS and\nECMWF are trying to push a slug of subtropical moisture east \ntoward the Desert Southwest next weekend. If the upper-low \npattern remains an open wave for the majority of next week, we \nwill see a strong zonal flow directing that moisture plume our \nway, possibly getting wrapped up ahead of the upper low as it \nswings into California. This potential heavy rainfall event is \ngetting picked up by the grand ensemble of the NBM, with a \n20-40 percent probability for a 48-hr rainfall in excess of an \ninch next Sunday and Monday (November 1-2) on the 23/12Z NBM \nrun. Again, still a lot of variance in the early stages of this \npattern setup, so my confidence is low on the timing and\nstrengthof any pcpn event we may see (not this weekend but the\nnext weekend). \n\nUntil then...enjoy the calm and take advantage of the beautiful\nweekend in store for Southeast Arizona.",
